  "body": "I've just noticed what looks like a minor bug in the specification. In the algorithm labeled [**get an XML encoding**](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#concept-get-xml-encoding-when-sniffing), step 3 defines _xmlDeclarationEnd_. I understand this to be intended to effectively truncate the byte stream at the closing bracket of the `<?xml>` tag, limiting where the `encoding` attribute can be found, but the algorithm as written never uses it in that way. In fact, this variable is never referenced after it's defined. The way it's written now, this snippet will set the character encoding to windows-1252:\r\n```\r\n<?xml /> encoding=\"windows-1252\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n(I've personally never seen an XML tag in an HTML file, so I don't know if this has ever happened in the wild. It strikes me as very unlikely, though.)\r\n\r\nI don't think this is the intended behavior, but if it is (e.g. for compatibility with existing content), there should be a note to clarify that. And, of course, if it isn't intended, I think it should be fixed.",
